data.content:
**What changed:** `InvoiceCalculator` now takes an optional `TimeProvider` (defaults to the real clock), so existing callers are unaffected.

**In tests**, use the built-in fake (add the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package to your test project):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.SetUtcNow(new DateTimeOffset(2024, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**
- `TimeProvider` is built into .NET 8+ — no custom `IClock` interface needed.
- The `= null` default means zero changes to production call sites.
- `FakeTimeProvider` gives you `SetUtcNow()` and `Advance()` out of the box.
